1. For-Each Loop
The for-each loop allows for iterating over elements of an array or collection.
public class ForEachExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        for (int number : numbers) {
            System.out.println(number);
        }
    }
}

2. Varargs (Variable Arguments)
Varargs allows passing a variable number of arguments to a method.
public class VarArgsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printNumbers(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
    }

    private static void printNumbers(int... numbers) {
        for (int number : numbers) {
            System.out.println(number);
        }
    }
}

3. Static Import
Static import allows importing static members (fields and methods) of a class directly.
import static java.lang.Math.*;

public class StaticImportExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double result = sqrt(25); // No need to use Math.sqrt
        System.out.println("Square root of 25: " + result);
    }
}

4. Autoboxing and Unboxing
Autoboxing automatically converts between primitive types and their corresponding wrapper classes.
public class AutoboxingExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Autoboxing: converting int to Integer
        Integer a = 10;
        
        // Unboxing: converting Integer to int
        int b = a;

        System.out.println("Autoboxed Integer: " + a);
        System.out.println("Unboxed int: " + b);
    }
}

5. Enums
Enums are a special type in Java that represent a group of constants.
public class EnumExample {
    enum Days {
        MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Days today = Days.WEDNESDAY;
        System.out.println("Today is: " + today);
    }
}

6. Covariant Return Type
Covariant return types allow an overridden method to return a subclass of the return type declared in the superclass.
class Animal {
    Animal get() {
        return this;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    Dog get() {
        return this;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Dog method");
    }
}

public class CovariantReturnExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Dog().get().display();
    }
}

7. Generics
Generics provide a way to define classes, interfaces, and methods with type parameters.
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class GenericsExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("Hello");
        list.add("World");

        for (String str : list) {
            System.out.println(str);
        }
    }
}

8. Annotations
Annotations provide metadata for your code. One of the built-in annotations is @Override.
class Parent {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent method");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Child method");
    }
}

public class AnnotationExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Child().display();
    }
}

